<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Cabaña</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1763–1774</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Fortaleza de San Carlos de la Cabaña</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Cabaña, the Fortaleza de San Carlos de la Cabaña, is an 18th-century fortress on the heights overlooking the entrance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> harbour in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> built it after the British capture of the city in 1762, and it was the largest Spanish fortification in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. It later served as a military prison, and in 1959 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/che-guevara">Che Guevara</a> made it his headquarters.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-cabana.jpg" alt="La Cabaña" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Entrance_to_fortress_La_Caba%C3%B1a,_Havana,_Cuba.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
